Host tests use PlatformIO Test Runner with Unity. Install PlatformIO Core and a
local C++ compiler (GCC or Clang), then run from the repository root:

    pio test -e native

PlatformIO installs the Native platform and Unity on the first run. No Arduino
board is required. Each test is reported separately; setUp resets the simulated
bus before each test. Firmware builds still default to nanoatmega328new, which
excludes this host-only suite.

For AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer on a supported host compiler:

    ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 pio test -e native_sanitized

Leak detection is disabled for ptrace-based environments. Address and undefined
behavior checks remain enabled.

The tests use the real Jtag.hpp implementation and compile JtagCommon.cpp with
simulated JtagGpio and GpioPin implementations and a minimal Arduino.h. They verify buffer packing,
validation, transmitted bits, received bits, partial-byte padding and raw output
bounds. Sequence tests record individual bus clocks; IR tests verify TAP
transitions, and reset is checked for five clocks with TMS high. They do not
verify GPIO timing or physical JTAG communication.
